---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { REPO_URL, absoluteUrl, pageTitle } from '../lib/seo';
import { highlightLines } from '../lib/highlight';

// Taken from the real facts test for "thenApply vs thenApplyAsync".
const example = `var f = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
    Facts.await(gate);
    return "fresh";
}, ioPool);
var ranOn = f.thenApply(r -> Thread.currentThread().getName());
gate.countDown();
assertEquals("io-1", ranOn.join());`;
const code = await highlightLines([example], 'java');
const description =
  'Every Threadline timeline has a runnable Java twin. CI runs it on JDK 21 and 25 and asserts facts about which thread ran what, never timings.';
const structuredData = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: 'How Threadline verifies every timeline',
  description,
  url: absoluteUrl('/how-we-verify/'),
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Bhumil', url: 'https://github.com/bhumil2210' },
};
---

<Base title={pageTitle('How We Verify Every Timeline')} {description} type="article" {structuredData}>
  <style is:inline set:html={code.css} />
  <article class="page">
    <h1>How we verify every timeline</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A wrong thread assignment teaches a wrong mental model. So no timeline on this site is drawn from memory: each one is
      checked against a real program, on every change and every week.
    </p>

    <h2>A runnable twin for every timeline</h2>
    <p>
      Every scenario and every what-if variant has a Java program that does exactly what the timeline shows, with the same
      code lines, in the same order. A test fails the build if a timeline has no twin, or if its code drifts from the twin.
    </p>

    <h2>Facts, not timings</h2>
    <p>
      Threads are scheduled differently on every machine, so the tests never check how long something took. They check
      facts that must hold on any machine: which thread ran a callback, which thread was <code>BLOCKED</code> while another
      held a lock, that a count lost an update. Gates and latches force the interesting interleaving, so a test can't pass
      by luck.
    </p>
    <p>For example, "a non-async <code>thenApply</code> runs on the thread that completed the future":</p>
    <pre class="code"><code set:html={code.lines[0]!.join('\n')} /></pre>

    <h2>Two JDKs, every week</h2>
    <p>
      GitHub Actions runs every test on JDK 21 and JDK 25, on every pull request and once a week. Some behavior really
      differs between versions: a virtual thread blocking inside <code>synchronized</code> is pinned to its carrier on JDK
      21 but not on JDK 25 (JEP 491), and the tests check both.
    </p>

    <h2>Gotchas are claims too</h2>
    <p>
      The warnings under each timeline are tested as well: that <code>newFixedThreadPool</code>'s queue is unbounded, that
      throwing <code>InterruptedException</code> clears the interrupt flag, that the common pool has one thread fewer than
      your cores.
    </p>

    <p class="source">
      All of it is open source: <a href={`${REPO_URL}/tree/main/verify/java`}>the verification programs on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/">Back to the scenarios</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    max-width: 70ch;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 48px 0 8px;
    font-size: clamp(28px, 5vw, 40px);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .lead {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 32px 0 8px;
    font-size: 19px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  :not(pre) > code {
    padding: 1px 5px;
    background: var(--surface-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 0.88em;
  }

  .code {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    padding: 12px 16px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    font: 13.5px/1.6 var(--font-mono);
  }

  a {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 10px 0;
    margin: -10px 0;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .source {
    margin-top: 32px;
  }
</style>
